Git Command Reference Cheat Sheet

Inspection:

git status — Check modified, staged, and untracked files.

git diff — View unstaged changes line by line.

git diff --staged — View staged changes awaiting commit.

git log --oneline — View a clean, condensed commit history.

Tracking & Staging:

git add <file> — Stage a specific file.

git add . — Stage all changes in the current directory.

git restore --staged <file> — Unstage a file while keeping your local edits.

Committing & Syncing:

git commit -m "message" — Save staged changes to local history.

git push origin <branch> — Upload local commits to the remote repository.

git pull origin <branch> — Fetch and merge remote changes into your local branch.

Branching & Context Switching:

git switch -c <branch> — Create and switch to a new branch.

git switch <branch> — Switch to an existing branch.

git stash — Temporarily shelve uncommitted work.

git stash pop — Reapply shelved changes.

Fixing Mistakes:

git restore <file> — Discard local unstaged changes in a file.

git revert <commit-hash> — Create a new commit that safely inverts a previous commit.